@echo off
:: install.bat - one-line installer wrapper for the Fa CLI on Windows cmd.exe.
::
:: This file is for users running from Command Prompt (cmd.exe). It delegates
:: to the PowerShell installer, which does the actual work:
::   1. Detects the Windows architecture.
::   2. Downloads the matching Fa binary from the latest GitHub Release.
::   3. Installs it to a directory on the user PATH.
::   4. Falls back to `dart pub global activate` if no binary is available.
::
:: Run from cmd:
::   curl -fsSL "https://fa1.dev/install.bat" -o install.bat && install.bat
::
:: Or directly from PowerShell:
::   irm https://fa1.dev/install.ps1 | iex
::
:: ===========================================================================
::  DO NOT EDIT THIS FILE BY HAND. It is generated from site/install-config.yaml
::  by `dart run scripts/gen_installers.dart`.
:: ===========================================================================

:: Keep this file CRLF-terminated and ASCII-only. cmd.exe seeks a batch file
:: by BYTE offset, so LF-only endings make it resume mid-line and execute
:: fragments of the next line - and multi-byte UTF-8 glyphs (box drawing, em
:: dashes) are what turn that mis-seek into command fragments. The generator
:: normalises endings and the generated header; keep the template clean too.

:: The banner and status glyphs are UTF-8; switch the console code page so
:: cmd.exe does not print them as mojibake.
chcp 65001 >nul

:: gh-1945: tell the PowerShell installer it is running on behalf of
:: cmd.exe, so its completion recipe prints cmd-compatible commands
:: (`irm` and `$env:` do not exist in cmd). Batch files run in-process,
:: so the flag is scoped with setlocal/endlocal: if the user interrupts
:: the install with Ctrl+C, the batch aborts before the explicit clear
:: below runs - cmd still discards the local frame, leaving the user's
:: session clean.
setlocal
set "FA_INSTALL_VIA=cmd"

powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "irm https://fa1.dev/install.ps1 | iex"

set "FA_INSTALL_VIA="
endlocal

:: A failed installer must not be announced as success: the wrapper used to
:: print "fa is ready" even when PowerShell aborted before downloading
:: anything. ERRORLEVEL survives endlocal, so it is still readable here.
if errorlevel 1 goto :fa_install_failed

:: Make fa usable in THIS console too: the user-PATH update above only takes
:: effect in NEW windows, and the child PowerShell's session refresh dies
:: with it. Batch files run in-process in cmd.exe, so this set persists.
:: LOCALAPPDATA is unset in some service-spawned shells - fall back to the
:: location the installer itself uses, so both agree.
set "FA_BIN=%LOCALAPPDATA%\Fa\bin"
if not defined LOCALAPPDATA set "FA_BIN=%USERPROFILE%\AppData\Local\Fa\bin"
set "PATH=%FA_BIN%;%PATH%"
echo.
echo fa is ready in THIS console. For other windows, open a NEW terminal.
exit /b 0

:fa_install_failed
echo.
echo Install failed - fa was NOT installed. Fix the error above, then run the
echo installer again.
exit /b 1
